#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("Northwind Switchgrass", "Panicum virgatum 'Northwind'", "switchgrass")[
  'Northwind' is one of the most architecturally striking native grasses available — strongly upright, wind-resistant, and reaching 5–6 feet tall with golden-yellow fall color. Excellent for year-round structure, privacy screening, and wildlife habitat. Unusual among ornamental grasses in tolerating clay soils. This is the most clay-tolerant grass in your landscape. Established 2+ years — on drip irrigation.
]

=== Watering

On drip irrigation with a *0.5 gph emitter*. See the Irrigation & Watering section for the shared drip schedule. Northwind Switchgrass is adaptable and tolerates both moderate drought and occasional wet periods in clay — it handles the shared schedule well at low emitter output. No supplemental irrigation needed once the plant goes dormant in fall.

=== Fertilization

Apply a light N-K slow-release fertilizer in mid-spring as growth resumes — use zero or near-zero phosphorus formula (your soil P is HIGH). Potassium sulfate (0-0-50) at low rate in fall improves cold hardiness and stem stiffness. Avoid high-nitrogen fertilizers — they cause the upright stems to flop and reduce fall color intensity.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Cut back to 4–6 inches in late winter to early spring (late February–March) before new growth emerges. Use loppers or a hedge trimmer for efficiency. Leave standing through winter — seed heads and stems provide wildlife food and four-season interest. 'Northwind' is one of the most structurally persistent grasses through Colorado winters.

=== Wildlife Value

Switchgrass is an outstanding multi-season wildlife plant. The seed heads produced in August–October are consumed by house finches, American robins, red-wing blackbirds, and common starlings. Northern flickers probe the base for insects in fall and winter. The tall, dense clumps provide nesting cover for red-wing blackbirds (which typically nest near the base of tall grasses) and winter shelter for house finches and sparrows. Cottontail rabbits are rarely interested in switchgrass. The clump structure also supports beneficial insect communities and provides chrysalis anchoring points for butterflies.

=== Pests & Disease

Switchgrass is virtually pest-free. Occasional #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=switchgrass+rust+treatment")[rust] can occur in wet conditions — it is rarely severe enough to require treatment. Smut diseases may affect seed heads in some years but do not threaten plant health. No serious insect pests target switchgrass in Colorado. This is one of the most trouble-free plants in the landscape.

=== Other Care

Unlike most ornamental grasses, 'Northwind' does not need to be divided for vigor — it improves in place over many years. If division is desired for propagation, do so in early spring before growth begins. This plant has a positive ecological presence: it supports beneficial insects, sequesters carbon, and provides structure during dormant seasons when most perennials have disappeared.
